      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "body": "> 5\\. > Otherwise, there could be a risk that random LLM bots try to send pulls with \"fixes\"?\n>         \n>         \n>         <img alt=\"Image\" width=\"1080\" height=\"632\" src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/5767891/684979169-80cefcdb-6e20-4fe0-803e-c99298f1d9bb.png?jwt=\">\n\nHeh, I understand that modern LLMs are capable, and that some projects are converging toward \"issues-are-the-new-prs\". Again, no objection if we want that as well, but I think issues would still need to be high quality and human-curated.\n\nI don't think we want a full LLM-issues + LLM-pulls workflow. Also, each LLM-pull needs a reviewer/mentor for remotely prompting the LLM (if needed). So to me it seems easier if the reviewer/mentor just used their own LLM to create the pull. Alternatively, there could be a projected-curated LLM that accepts prompts from contributors. However fanning stuff out to random OpenClaw instances often eats reviewer/mentor capacity without resulting in a merge anyway. At least that is my impression. Recall that a good chunk of Monday morning this week was spent by several reviewers to close 10+ irrelevant LLM-pulls.",
