      "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:57:28Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:57:28Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "I think \"LLM that accepts prompts from contributors\" is a good idea\ncompared to the alternative where drive-by users submit text already\ngenerated by LLMs. It would be even better if drive-by users could\ncontribute credits for LLMs instead of contributing token outputs.\nSomething odd about this thread, though, is that Mr. Sanders is not a\ndrive-by contributor...\n\n(While I'm ranting about this, I think the framing that makes sense here is\nthat these and other open-source software projects are human projects with\nhuman collaborators. People can use AI tools, but they shouldn't expect\nthese LLM tools to be first-class collaborators with the humans. Nor does\nusing these tools make you an active collaborator. The only way to become a\ncollaborator is to actually collaborate with at least one other person on\nthe project. Collaboration isn't just posting an issue or a pull request,\nbut actually getting involved in the project and understanding what's going\non and how people are working together. You have to actually work with at\nleast one other human being in the project, unless your goal was just to\npost text out of sense of vanity. People can collaborate with any person\nthey want, but there's no way to force them into a relationship with an AI\ntool that they don't want to work with.)\n",
